":"skill"},{"name":"skill:mcp-integration","description":"Wire an MCP server into a plugin or config. Use when adding or configuring an MCP server, editing .mcp.json, connecting an external service, or choosing between stdio, SSE, HTTP and WebSocket transports.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:memory-leak-debugging","description":"Diagnoses and resolves memory leaks in JavaScript/Node.js applications. Use when a user reports high memory usage, OOM errors, or wants to analyze heapsnapshots or run memory leak detection tools like memlab.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:morning-briefing","description":"Use when asked for a morning briefing, daily briefing, daily Optimizely digest, 'what do I need to know today', or a video briefing: collects Navid's Optimizely GitHub, Jira, Confluence, Email and Teams activity (read-only), renders a narrated 5-10 minute presentation video (local TTS) plus a Lavish page with in-page reply questions, and relays his answers.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:no-mistakes","description":"Validate your code changes through the no-mistakes pipeline - automated code review, tests, lint, docs, push, PR, and CI - before they reach the configured push target. Use when the user asks to run no-mistakes, gate or ship or validate their changes, push safely, asks you to do a task and then validate it, or invokes /no-mistakes.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:oh-my-issues","description":"Cluster a GitHub issue backlog by root cause into a small set of plan-master issues, redirect children with a standardized comment, and bundle architectural-fix PRs that close clusters atomically. Use when an issue tracker has accumulated dozens of reports that share underlying defects, when asked to triage / consolidate / cluster / dedupe issues, when asked to build a plan series or roadmap from open issues, or when routing a new incoming bug into an existing plan.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:omp-browser-default","description":"Default recipe for the omp browser (xd://browser): headless-first, dialogs:\"accept\" on every open, the relay used only as a credential source to transplant a logged-in session, parallel tabs across subagents, and screenshot-backed QA reports. Use whenever driving a browser, verifying a web UI, handling a native alert/confirm/prompt/\"Leave site?\" dialog, or needing an authenticated session without a password or Duo prompt.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:opal-connect","description":"Reach Opal localdev in a browser on the Mac when the stack runs on the cmp-ec2 VPS, and start or stop it there. Use when the user says \"connect to opal\", \"open opal\", \"run opal on the vps\", \"start opal localdev\", \"stop opal on the vm\", or when opal-localdev.optimizely.com / opal-backend-localdev.optimizely.com:8443 / ws.opal-localdev.optimizely.com fails to load, times out, refuses the connection, or shows a cert error.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:opal-devtools","description":"Hit Opal service APIs, view logs, and run tests in localdev","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:opal-vps-auth","description":"Run an authenticated omp browser against opal-localdev on the cmp-ec2 VPS by transplanting the Mac's logged-in Okta session — no Duo tap on the VPS. Use when asked to open/test/QA opal on the VPS, seed or refresh the VPS opal session, watch for its expiry, or drive the omp browser against opal-localdev.optimizely.com from cmp-ec2. Also covers watching/steering a VPS omp agent via a collab host.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:open-worktree","description":"Create or open a git worktree from the latest origin/main and spin up an isolated Docker stack for it","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:opencode-config","description":"Configure opencode itself. Use when editing opencode.jsonc, adding an MCP server, changing agent models or permissions, wiring a plugin or LSP, or when asked why a setting is not taking effect.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:openrouter-best-free","description":"Find the top-performing free OpenRouter model by coding benchmark and set it as the OMP default model. /openrouter-best-free on switches to the best free coding model; /openrouter-best-free off restores the previous default.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:optimizely-vps","description":"Connection facts and layout for Navid's Optimizely remote development VPS (EC2 localdev box). Use when work should run on the remote dev box rather than the MacBook, when SSHing to the VPS, when running CMP or Opal localdev, when the user says \"on the vps\", \"remote machine\", \"dev box\", \"cmp-ec2\", or when you need to know which machine you are currently on.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:orca-cli","description":"Use the public `orca` CLI to operate Orca-managed worktrees, folder contexts, terminals, repos, automations, artifacts, skill sharing, worktree comments, and the browser embedded inside the Orca app. Use when the user says \"$orca-cli\", \"use orca cli\", \"Orca worktree\", \"child worktree\", \"cardStatus\", \"spawn codex/claude in a worktree\", \"read/wait/send Orca terminal\", \"terminal send\", \"full handoff\", \"handover\", \"give this to another agent\", \"another worktree\", \"Orca browser\", \"orca artifacts\", \"share HTML/Markdown\", \"public artifact link\", \"share skills\", or \"control the browser inside Orca\". Prefer this over raw `git worktree`, ad hoc PTYs, Playwright, or Computer Use when the task touches Orca-managed state. Use Computer Use for external browser windows, webviews, or desktop UI only when the task requires OS/window-level control such as focus, menus, dialogs, coordinates, or screenshots. Use `orca-cli` for Orca's embedded pages and a page-automation tool such as Playwright or CDP for external pages.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:orchestration","description":"Coordinate supervised Orca workers: threaded messages, blocking ask/reply, task dispatch, worker_done/escalation waits, task DAGs, decision gates, coordinator loops, and decomposing work across agents. Use `orca-cli` for full ownership handoffs — \"hand off\", \"handoff\", \"handover\", \"give this to another agent\", \"another worktree\" — unless asked to supervise, monitor, or coordinate a DAG, and for terminal control, lightweight terminal prompts, shell commands, Orca worktree management, and reading or waiting on terminals. Use Computer Use for external browser windows, webviews, Orca app UI, or desktop UI outside Orca's embedded browser only when the task requires OS/window-level control such as focus, menus, dialogs, coordinates, or screenshots. Use `orca-cli` for Orca's embedded pages and a page-automation tool such as Playwright or CDP for external pages.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:pi-messenger-crew","description":"Orchestrator reference for pi-messenger Crew planning, task management, configuration, and agent coordination. Crew workers already have the pi_messenger actions they need in crew-worker.md.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:plugin-settings","description":"Store per-project plugin configuration. Use when a plugin needs user-configurable settings, when working with .local.md state files, or when reading YAML frontmatter as config.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:plugin-structure","description":"Scaffold or organise a plugin. Use when creating a plugin, setting up plugin.json, arranging commands, agents, skills and hooks, or fixing plugin auto-discovery.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:pr-checkout-shadman","description":"Checkout a PR, review its diff locally, and optionally mount affected services into localdev via worktree. Use when asked to review a PR, check out a PR, or look at a PR's changes.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:pr-demo-comment","description":"Publish or refresh a single \"How to test this PR\" comment on GitHub. Use when asked to demo a PR to reviewers, post how-to-test steps, or attach QA screenshots that must render in a private repo.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:prepare-for-review","description":"Use when Navid says \"prepare for review\", \"prep this PR/ticket for review\", or asks to get someone's PRs ready for him to review. Produces a visual context + decision-path page plus a full review-kit review per PR.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:project-artifact","description":"Structured artifact generation with a promotion gate. Prevents silent overwrites of human-approved canonical files. Use when producing long-lived artifacts (tech specs, reports, diagrams) that need explicit approval before becoming canonical.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:proteus-python","description":"Write Proteus action card UI resources for Opal tool providers using the Python SDK. Use when creating @resource handlers, building UI.Document templates, or adding ui_resource to @tool definitions.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:replies-and-reports","description":"Structure a reply, report, nudge, or a document a human will read to make a decision. Use when writing a main-thread answer, a final report, a PR/Jira/chat comment, a .md the user asked for so they can see/review/decide, or when a response is getting long and needs a visual, a tag line, or a .tmp/ overflow file.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:rk-converge-review","description":"Run an AI code review that terminates, and decide whether a PR may merge. Use when reviewing a pull request, when asked to \"review it again\" or \"are the findings fixed\", when deciding whether a PR can merge, or when an AI review loop keeps finding new things every round.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:rk-map-phase","description":"Establish the surface area of a change before anything else happens, and freeze it. Use at the start of a ticket, when asked what a change will touch, before writing a spec, when deciding what to read, or when tempted to grep the source tree to find out where something lives.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:rk-memory-bank","description":"Find where something lives and what a change will break, before writing any code. Use when starting a feature, when asking where something lives, when planning a change, when working out what a change will break, or when planning QA coverage.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:rk-ticket-flow","description":"Drive a ticket from first read to mergeable through four gates that are scripts, not self-assessments. Use when starting or picking up a ticket, when planning a feature, when asked \"can I start implementing yet\", when asked \"are you done\" or \"are you confident\", and when getting a change ready to merge.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:rk-workflow","description":"Drive a whole feature — an idea, not a ticket — from first read to a reviewed PR by decomposing it into tickets and running each one through rk-ticket-flow. Use when starting end-to-end feature development, when an idea is too large for one ticket, when asked to plan and ship a feature, or when the pre/post diagram discipline is needed for a PR.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:run","description":"Launch this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, demo, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app rather than only in tests.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:sentry-jira-reconcile","description":"Loop-friendly reconciler that pulls unresolved + unassigned Sentry issues for one or more projects, files Jira tickets for the ones that don't have one yet (priority scaled by impact), and assigns the Sentry issue to its owning team. Designed to be re-run until the queue converges to zero. Defaults to Opal apps; configurable via args.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:session-report","description":"Produce an explorable HTML report of session usage. Use when asked where tokens went, which prompts were expensive, how much a session cost, or for a breakdown of subagent and skill usage.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:simplify","description":"Clean up changed code for reuse, simplicity, and altitude. Use after implementing something, when asked to tidy or simplify a diff, or when a change works but looks over-complicated. Quality only, not bug-hunting.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:skill-creator","description":"Create new skills, modify and improve existing skills, and measure skill performance. Use when users want to create a skill from scratch, edit, or optimize an existing skill, run evals to test a skill, benchmark skill performance with variance analysis, or optimize a skill's description for better triggering accuracy.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:skill-development","description":"Author or improve a skill. Use when creating a new skill, editing an existing one, rewriting a skill description so it triggers reliably, or organising skill content with progressive disclosure.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:standalone-scheduled-sessions","description":"Use when investigating recurring OpenCode sessions, scheduled jobs, background routines, /schedule-standalone-session, ScheduleCreate, schedule_job, every-N-minutes runs, or cleanup of durable schedules.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:standup","description":"Facilitate a read-only standup across git worktrees, branches, or PRs to compare changes and produce one consolidation plan. Use when comparing what diverged across worktrees/branches/PRs and deciding how to consolidate them. NOT for Superset agent activity — if the question is 'what did my Superset agents do' or 'summarise my workspaces, tasks and agent terminals', use the separate `superset-standup` skill instead.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:subagent-drift-watch","description":"Periodically check running subagents for drift or stall without interrupting them - read transcript tails on a cadence, course-correct only the ones that need it. Use when several subagents are running in parallel and the main thread is waiting on them.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:superset-10x","description":"Personalized audit that teaches the advanced Superset features the user isn't using yet (automations, parallel agents, tasks, multi-host, terminal remote control, custom commands, MCP) and sets them up live. Use when the user wants to get more out of Superset, asks \"what else can Superset do\", \"how do I 10x my workflow\", \"what am I missing\", or wants to learn a specific Superset feature.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:superset-automate","description":"Turn a recurring chore into a Superset automation. Drafts the agent prompt, confirms schedule and target, creates it with the CLI, and reviews the first run together. Use when the user wants a scheduled or recurring agent, a daily or weekly job, or says things like \"every morning do X\", \"automate this\", \"set up a cron agent\", \"run this on a schedule\".","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:superset-browser","description":"Open, navigate, screenshot, read, click, and type in web pages from an agent. Use when the user asks to open a URL, preview or verify a running web app, check a page's console, fill a form, click through a web flow, or automate anything in a browser, including \"open localhost:3000\", \"screenshot the page\", \"what does the console say\", \"click the submit button\". Drives the in-app browser pane of a Superset workspace by default and Browser Use for browsers the panes cannot reach.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:superset-computer","description":"Operate the user's real desktop apps and windows on macOS, Windows, or Linux. Use when the user asks to open or drive a native app, click or type in a desktop UI, inspect or arrange a window, use their signed-in system browser, or verify an end-to-end GUI flow, including \"open Settings and turn on dark mode\", \"click Save in that window\", \"what's on screen\". Uses an accessibility-first driver (Cua Driver by default, Peekaboo on macOS). Not for Superset's in-app browser pane, headless scraping, or anything an API or CLI can do directly.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:superset-contribute","description":"Set up a Superset open-source contribution, from forking and cloning superset-sh/superset through local dev setup and the repo's rules to a merge-ready PR. Use when the user wants to contribute to Superset, fix a Superset bug themselves, add a feature to Superset, or prepare a PR against superset-sh/superset.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:superset-doctor","description":"Diagnose and fix Superset problems such as connection failures, offline hosts, terminals not attaching, auth or update issues. Use when the user reports something broken or misbehaving in Superset itself (\"Superset won't connect\", \"my host shows offline\", \"terminal won't attach\", \"the app is stuck\"), before filing feedback.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skil