"seance\"` with `sourceSession` (id/path); optional `model` overrides the saved source model. The source stays unchanged.\nIf the saved model is absent/unavailable, set `model` explicitly.\n\n`tools`: eval-defined, run in your kernel.\n`effort`: `\"lo\"`|`\"med\"`|`\"hi\"` by how open-ended the problem is.\nChildren start blank; parent IRC steers immediately; large payloads via `local://<path>`, NEVER inline.\n\n# Format\n`context`: shared (`# Goal`, `# Contract` interfaces); NEVER repeat per task.\n`task`: self-contained (`# Target` files/non-goals, `# Change` steps/APIs, `# Acceptance` observable result).\n\n# Available Agents\n- `junior-software-engineer`: Use this agent for simple, well-scoped, and clearly defined engineering subtasks. This agent is best for boilerplate code, test scaffolding, simple CRUD operations, formatting/linting fixes, straightforward helper functions, and other low-risk tasks where the requirements are unambiguous. It always reports results back to the software-engineer who assigned the task and does NOT make independent architectural decisions.\n\nExamples:\n\n<example>\nContext: Software engineer needs test scaffolding.\nuser: \"Set up the test file structure and boilerplate for the PaymentService tests\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the junior-software-engineer agent to scaffold the test file following existing patterns.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Software engineer needs a simple helper.\nuser: \"Write a utility function to format currency amounts with locale support\"\nassistant: \"I'll delegate this well-defined utility to the junior-software-engineer agent.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Software engineer needs repetitive boilerplate.\nuser: \"Add the standard request/response DTOs for the new UserActivity endpoint\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the junior-software-engineer agent to generate the DTO classes following existing conventions.\"\n</example>\n- `ops-debugger`: Use this agent when you need to investigate logs (Datadog, GCP Cloud Logging, docker logs), debug Docker container issues, fetch external ticket information from Jira/Confluence, or perform large data pull/push operations that require many tool calls but low analytical complexity. This agent is ideal for grunt work that involves many sequential operations but doesn't require deep architectural thinking.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: The user wants to investigate why a service is failing in localdev.\\nuser: \"primary-agent keeps crashing, can you check what's going on?\"\\nassistant: \"Let me use the ops-debugger agent to pull the logs and investigate the crash.\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince the user needs log investigation and Docker debugging, use the Agent tool to launch the ops-debugger agent to check container logs and status.\\n</commentary>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: The user needs context from a Jira ticket before starting work.\\nuser: \"Pull all the details from OPAL-1234 and its linked tickets\"\\nassistant: \"I'll use the ops-debugger agent to fetch the ticket and all linked ticket details.\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince the user needs external ticket fetching which involves multiple API calls, use the Agent tool to launch the ops-debugger agent to pull all the Jira data.\\n</commentary>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: The user wants to check Datadog for error patterns.\\nuser: \"Check Datadog for any 500 errors in hypatia over the last hour\"\\nassistant: \"Let me launch the ops-debugger agent to search Datadog logs for those errors.\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince this is a log investigation task, use the Agent tool to launch the ops-debugger agent to query Datadog.\\n</commentary>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: The user needs to pull data from multiple Confluence pages.\\nuser: \"Find all the Confluence docs related to code execution architecture\"\\nassistant: \"I'll use the ops-debugger agent to search and pull those Confluence documents.\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince this involves multiple search and fetch operations across Confluence, use the Agent tool to launch the ops-debugger agent for the bulk data retrieval.\\n</commentary>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: The user is working on a feature and a service goes down mid-development.\\nuser: \"tools-mgmt-service just stopped responding, help\"\\nassistant: \"Let me launch the ops-debugger agent to check the container status and logs.\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince a Docker service is unresponsive and needs debugging, proactively use the Agent tool to launch the ops-debugger agent to diagnose the issue.\\n</commentary>\\n</example>\n- `qa-engineer`: Use this agent for frontend QA, browser-based testing, UI verification, and end-to-end flow validation. This agent uses the omp browser (`xd://browser`, headless-first) to verify that UI changes work correctly, check visual rendering, test user flows, and catch frontend regressions.\n\nExamples:\n\n<example>\nContext: Software engineer needs to verify a UI change.\nuser: \"Check that the new settings panel renders correctly and the save button works\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the qa-engineer agent to open the browser, navigate to the settings panel, and verify the UI.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Need to verify a full user flow.\nuser: \"Test the login flow end-to-end — enter credentials, submit, verify redirect\"\nassistant: \"I'll launch the qa-engineer agent to run through the login flow in the browser.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Checking for visual regressions.\nuser: \"Make sure the chat interface still looks right after the CSS changes\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the qa-engineer agent to take screenshots and verify the chat UI.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Accessibility check.\nuser: \"Check if the new modal has proper focus management and ARIA labels\"\nassistant: \"I'll delegate to the qa-engineer agent to run accessibility checks on the modal.\"\n</example>\n- `senior-qa-engineer`: Use this agent for expert-level QA: BDD/ATDD scenario design, spec testability validation, deep test plan creation, and guiding QA Engineer through test execution. This agent is the escalation peer to senior-software-engineer for quality assurance.\n\nExamples:\n\n<example>\nContext: Tech Spec needs BDD/ATDD scenario validation before implementation.\nuser: \"Review this tech spec and create detailed BDD/ATDD scenarios covering all use-cases and edge cases\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the senior-qa-engineer agent to collaborate with Tech Spec Architect on scenario design and validate testability.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: QA Engineer is stuck on test execution and needs guidance.\nuser: \"QA Engineer is confused about what this scenario actually expects — help clarify\"\nassistant: \"I'll escalate to senior-qa-engineer for expert guidance on scenario interpretation and test execution.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Test scenarios need completeness review.\nuser: \"Validate that these test scenarios cover happy paths, edge cases, error handling, security, integration, data integrity, and regression risks\"\nassistant: \"I'll use senior-qa-engineer with /multi-angle-verify and /multi-angle-review to audit scenario completeness.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Phase 4 QA execution revealed gaps in spec.\nuser: \"QA found missing scenarios — iterate with Tech Spec Architect to refine spec and close gaps\"\nassistant: \"I'll use senior-qa-engineer to loop back to Phase 1, work with Tech Spec Architect on refinements, and prevent implementation rework.\"\n</example>\n- `senior-software-engineer`: Use this agent when you need expert-level software engineering guidance, architecture decisions, code implementation, debugging complex issues, or technical leadership on development tasks. This agent also serves as the final verifier for complex or risky work produced by the software-engineer. Only escalate to this agent for work that truly needs senior-level judgment — architectural decisions, security-sensitive reviews, unblocking stuck engineers, and verification of complex changes.\n\nExamples:\n\n<example>\nContext: User needs to implement a new feature requiring architectural decisions.\nuser: \"I need to add a caching layer to our API to reduce database load\"\nassistant: \"Let me use the senior-software-engineer agent to help design and implement an appropriate caching strategy.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Software engineer is stuck on a complex issue.\nuser: \"The software-engineer couldn't resolve the deadlock — escalating for guidance\"\nassistant: \"I'll engage the senior-software-engineer agent to diagnose the deadlock and provide a solution approach.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: Complex work needs final verification.\nuser: \"Verify the new auth middleware implementation before we ship it\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the senior-software-engineer agent to review this security-sensitive code.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: User needs guidance on technical approach.\nuser: \"Should we use microservices or a monolith for our new project?\"\nassistant: \"Let me bring in the senior-software-engineer agent to analyze the tradeoffs and provide a recommendation based on your specific requirements.\"\n</example>\n- `software-engineer`: Use this agent for standard software engineering tasks: implementing features, fixing bugs, writing tests, refactoring code, and reviewing PRs. This is the default engineering agent for most day-to-day work. It coordinates the full team: delegates simple code tasks to junior-software-engineer, data-heavy investigation to ops-debugger, frontend QA to qa-engineer, and escalates architectural complexity to senior-software-engineer. Simple work is self-verified; complex or risky work is submitted to senior for approval.\n\nExamples:\n\n<example>\nContext: User needs a new endpoint implemented.\nuser: \"Add a REST endpoint to fetch paginated user activity logs\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the software-engineer agent to implement this endpoint, delegating simple helpers to junior and getting senior review for the architectural decisions.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: User reports a bug that needs log investigation.\nuser: \"Fix the race condition in the job queue processor — check Datadog for related errors\"\nassistant: \"I'll engage the software-engineer agent. It will delegate log investigation to ops-debugger and implement the fix, escalating to senior if the root cause is complex.\"\n</example>\n\n<example>\nContext: User needs a feature built and tested end-to-end.\nuser: \"Build the new settings panel and verify it works in the browser\"\nassistant: \"I'll use the software-engineer agent to implement the feature, delegate boilerplate to junior, and send qa-engineer to verify it in the browser.\"\n</example>\n- `tech-spec-architect`: Use this agent when the user requests creation of a technical specification document, needs architectural guidance for a new feature or system, wants to document technical design decisions, or when implementation questions arise that require architectural clarification. This agent should be invoked for planning phases before implementation begins, or when engineers need clarity on technical approaches.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: User requests a tech spec for a new feature.\\nuser: \"I need a tech spec for implementing user authentication with OAuth2\"\\nassistant: \"I'll use the tech-spec-architect agent to create a comprehensive technical specification for the OAuth2 authentication implementation.\"\\n<Task tool invocation to launch tech-spec-architect agent>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: A senior engineer has questions during implementation that need architectural clarification.\\nuser: \"The engineer implementing the payment system is asking whether we should use synchronous or asynchronous processing for refunds\"\\nassistant: \"I'll consult the tech-spec-architect agent to provide architectural guidance on the refund processing approach and update the tech spec accordingly.\"\\n<Task tool invocation to launch tech-spec-architect agent>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: User wants to document a system design before development starts.\\nuser: \"We need to design and document the microservices architecture for our new e-commerce platform\"\\nassistant: \"I'll engage the tech-spec-architect agent to create a detailed technical specification document outlining the microservices architecture for the e-commerce platform.\"\\n<Task tool invocation to launch tech-spec-architect agent>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nContext: User needs to update an existing tech spec based on implementation feedback.\\nuser: \"The team found that the proposed database schema won't scale. Can you revise the tech spec?\"\\nassistant: \"I'll use the tech-spec-architect agent to revise the schema design and update the tech spec with the new approach.\"\\n<Task tool invocation to launch tech-spec-architect agent>\\n</example>\n- `security-reviewer`: Read-only security specialist for evidence-backed repository vulnerability discovery\n- `seance` (READ-ONLY; investigation only, no edits): Read a historical session and nested transcripts to answer a consult.\n- `task`: General-purpose subagent with full capabilities for delegated multi-step tasks","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"context":{"type":"string"},"tasks":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"task":{"type":"string"},"solutionSpace":{"type":"string"},"name":{"type":"string"},"agent":{"type":"string","default":"task"},"effort":{"enum":["lo","med","hi"],"type":"string"},"tools":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"sourceSession":{"type":"string"},"model":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}]}},"required":["task","solutionSpace"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["context","tasks"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"wait","description":"Wait only when blocked wi